      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-224981",
      ":question/problem": "After collecting information over the last few months, a plumbing company manager determined that, on average, the company served $6$ clients daily. After performing an advertising campaign, the manager decided to conduct a statistical hypothesis test at the $1%$ significance level to see whether there had been an increase in the number of clients served daily.\n\nThe manager randomly selected a particular day and found that $13$ clients were served that day. He compared this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, using the cumulative distribution table below, where $X\u223cPo(6)$.\n\n| $x$ | $10$ | $11$ | $12$ | $13$ | $14$ | $15$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.9574$ | $0.9799$ | $0.9912$ | $0.9964$ | $0.9986$ | $0.9995$ |\n\nBy assuming that every client is served independently, fill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of clients served on a randomly selected day, then the test statistic is\n$X = 13$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} Since $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $1%$, there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.Therefore, we {{field-6}} that the number of clients served per day has increased since the plumbing company performed an advertising campaign."
    },
