      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-225039",
      ":question/problem": "A travel agency sells, on average, $5$ tourist packages per week. The agency decided to expand its offer of tourist destinations in response to customer suggestions. The director of the travel agency conducted a hypothesis test at the $1%$ significance level to test whether the sales increased.\n\nThe agency director noticed that $7$ tourist packages were sold during a week after the offer of tourist destinations was expanded. He compared this experimental evidence against a null hypothesis, making use of the cumulative distribution table below, where $X\u223cPo(5)$.\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $P(X \\le x)$ | $0.6160$ | $0.7622$ | $0.8666$ | $0.9319$ | $0.9682$ | $0.9863$ |\n\nAssuming that sales occur independently, fill in the missing information from the following statements.\n\nIf $X$ represents the number of travel packages sold during a randomly selected week, then the test statistic is\n$X \\ge 5$ {{field-1}} {{field-2}} Since $P($ {{field-3}} $)$ {{field-4}} $1%$, there is {{field-5}} evidence to reject $H_{0}$.Therefore, we {{field-6}} that the number of tourist packages sold per week has increased after the travel agency expanded the offer of tourist destinations."
